course: dictionary
lesson: 7250
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which one you need. «At the table you wait until everyone is served, then ask before taking the last piece.» Here a piece is one part of the food, like a slice of cake. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: «In the late 1950s engineers invented the microchip, which put many tiny parts onto a small piece of silicon.» Here piece means one single item, a small bit of material. The clues are tiny and silicon, not breaking or cutting.
explain: Piece is also one of the parts of a game. «White has taken 5 pieces and Black 3: 32 − 5 − 3 = 24 pieces are still on the board.» The words taken and board tell you the pieces are the counters in a game like chess.
fact: A piece is a part of something that has been cut, broken or divided, like a piece of cake.
fact: A piece can be one single item, like a piece of paper or a piece of advice.
fact: A piece can be a work of music, art or writing.
fact: «Piece together» means to join parts to make a whole, and «in one piece» means not broken or harmed.
fact: «Piece of cake» means something very easy.
exercise: «At the table, ask before taking the last piece.» What does piece mean here? | *One part of the food | A game of cards | A work of music | A chess piece
exercise: «A small piece of silicon» holds many tiny parts in a microchip. What does piece mean here? | A painting | *One single item, a small bit of material | A part broken off a cake | A tune
exercise: What does «piece together» mean? | To break something into bits | *To join parts to make a whole | To play a game of chess | To cook a meal
test: «The vase fell to pieces when it hit the floor.» What does fall to pieces mean? | Turned into a bird | Was painted blue | Became very tall | *Broke into many parts
test: In a board game, a chess piece is what? | A kind of food | A type of hat | *One of the parts you move on the board | A piece of music
exam: «It was a piece of cake.» What does this phrase mean? | Very late | *Very easy | Very heavy | Very sweet
